<section class="friction-section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <h2>Understand Execution Friction</h2>
    <p>When an action does not happen, investigate the conditions before assuming the problem is a lack of motivation.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="friction-panel">
    <div class="friction-column">
      <h3>Common Sources of Friction</h3>
      <p>Friction is any condition that makes an intended action harder to start, continue, or complete.</p>
      <div class="friction-list">
        <div class="friction-item"><b>01</b><span><strong>Unclear next step:</strong> the task has no defined starting behavior.</span></div>
        <div class="friction-item"><b>02</b><span><strong>Excessive scope:</strong> the first step is too large for the available time.</span></div>
        <div class="friction-item"><b>03</b><span><strong>Missing resources:</strong> tools, information, or materials are unavailable.</span></div>
        <div class="friction-item"><b>04</b><span><strong>Competing demands:</strong> interruptions or obligations take priority.</span></div>
        <div class="friction-item"><b>05</b><span><strong>Emotional resistance:</strong> uncertainty, discomfort, or fear makes initiation harder.</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="friction-column">
      <h3>How to Reduce Friction</h3>
      <p>Choose a practical adjustment that changes the conditions around the behavior.</p>
      <div class="friction-list">
        <div class="friction-item"><b>A</b><span>Write the first physical action in one sentence.</span></div>
        <div class="friction-item"><b>B</b><span>Reduce the task until it fits the time available.</span></div>
        <div class="friction-item"><b>C</b><span>Prepare the materials before the planned start time.</span></div>
        <div class="friction-item"><b>D</b><span>Protect a realistic work window from avoidable interruptions.</span></div>
        <div class="friction-item"><b>E</b><span>Use a manageable first step while preserving the larger goal.</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="review-section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <h2>Review the Result, Not Just the Effort</h2>
    <p>A useful review turns an experience into information that can improve the next attempt.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="review-grid">
    <article class="review-card">
      <span>REVIEW 01</span>
      <h3>What did I complete?</h3>
      <p>Record a visible result: a finished exercise, a decision, a draft, a comparison, or a prepared resource.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="review-card">
      <span>REVIEW 02</span>
      <h3>What made it easier or harder?</h3>
      <p>Identify the condition that influenced the action. Look for missing information, interruptions, or excessive scope.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="review-card">
      <span>REVIEW 03</span>
      <h3>What should change next time?</h3>
      <p>Keep what worked. Change one relevant condition and test whether the next attempt improves.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="lesson-panel">
  <details>
    <summary>
      <span class="chevron">▶</span>
      <span>Deep Lesson: The Difference Between Motivation, Activation & Momentum</span>
    </summary>

    <div class="lesson-content">
      <h3>Motivation is not the only variable controlling behavior.</h3>

      <p>
        A person can genuinely want an outcome and still fail to initiate
        the behavior required to reach it. The gap can come from unclear
        instructions, excessive task size, competing demands, poor timing,
        missing resources, or an environment that makes the undesired behavior
        easier.
      </p>

      <h4>1. Motivation</h4>
      <p>
        Motivation concerns the desire or willingness to pursue an outcome.
        It can fluctuate throughout the day, which means a system that depends
        entirely on feeling motivated is inherently unstable.
      </p>

      <h4>2. Activation</h4>
      <p>
        Activation is the transition from intention to the first observable
        behavior. "Study programming" is an intention. "Open the lesson and
        complete the first exercise" is an activation sequence.
      </p>

      <h4>3. Momentum</h4>
      <p>
        Momentum is the advantage created when one completed behavior makes
        another behavior easier to identify or initiate. For example, organizing
        your workspace can make tomorrow's study session easier to start.
      </p>

      <h4>4. The Important Distinction</h4>
      <p>
        A micro-action should not become an excuse to permanently avoid difficult
        work. Its purpose is to create an entry point. Once the system has
        established movement, the appropriate next step may be significantly larger.
      </p>

      <h4>5. The Better Question</h4>
      <p>
        Instead of asking, "How can I finish my entire goal today?" ask:
        <strong>What action would meaningfully change the state of the system
        within the time and capacity I actually have?</strong>
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

